16. TIMERS PHASE
----------------
setTimeout() aur setInterval() ke callbacks timers phase se associated hote hain.

Example:

setTimeout(() => {
    console.log("Timer");
}, 1000);


17. POLL PHASE
--------------
Poll phase I/O related callbacks ko process karta hai.

Example:
- File I/O
- Network I/O related callbacks


18. CHECK PHASE
---------------
Check phase mein setImmediate() ke callbacks execute hote hain.

Example:

setImmediate(() => {
    console.log("Immediate");
});


19. CLOSE CALLBACKS
-------------------
Closed resources ke callbacks yahan handle ho sakte hain.

Example:
Socket close hone ke baad related close callback.


20. setTimeout() VS setImmediate()
----------------------------------
Example:

setTimeout(() => {
    console.log("Timeout");
}, 0);

setImmediate(() => {
    console.log("Immediate");
});

Main module ke context mein order deterministic nahi maana jaana chahiye.

Lekin I/O callback ke andar setImmediate() normally pehle execute hota hai:

const fs = require("fs");

fs.readFile("data.txt", () => {

    setTimeout(() => {
        console.log("Timeout");
    }, 0);

    setImmediate(() => {
        console.log("Immediate");
    });

});

Typical output:

Immediate
Timeout

Reason:
I/O callback ke baad Event Loop check phase tak pahunchta hai jahan setImmediate() execute hota hai.


21. process.nextTick()
----------------------
Node.js mein process.nextTick() callback ko high-priority queue mein schedule karta hai.

Example:

process.nextTick(() => {
    console.log("Next Tick");
});

Simplified priority idea:

Current JavaScript
      ↓
process.nextTick queue
      ↓
Promise microtasks
      ↓
Event Loop phases

Caution:
Bahut saare recursive nextTick() callbacks Event Loop ko starve kar sakte hain.


22. PROMISE / MICROTASK
----------------------
Example:

console.log("Start");

Promise.resolve().then(() => {
    console.log("Promise");
});

console.log("End");

Output:

Start
End
Promise

Promise callbacks microtask queue mein schedule hote hain.

Simplified:

Current JavaScript
      ↓
Microtasks
      ↓
Event Loop phases


23. WORKER THREADS
------------------
Worker Threads Node.js mein CPU-intensive JavaScript work ko separate threads par run karne ke liye use kiye ja sakte hain.

Main Thread
     │
     ├── Event Loop
     │
     └── Worker Thread
             │
             └── CPU-intensive task

Useful for:
- Heavy calculations
- CPU-intensive processing
- Large computations

Worker Threads aur libuv Thread Pool same cheez nahi hain.


24. CPU-BOUND VS I/O-BOUND
--------------------------
I/O-bound tasks:
- Database
- File
- Network
- APIs

Node.js I/O-bound workloads ke liye bahut suitable hai because non-blocking I/O concurrency ko efficiently handle karta hai.

CPU-bound tasks:
- Huge calculations
- Complex computation
- Heavy data processing

Agar CPU-heavy JavaScript main thread par continuously chale:

CPU-heavy task
      ↓
Main Thread busy
      ↓
Event Loop blocked
      ↓
Other requests delayed

Aise cases mein Worker Threads useful ho sakte hain.


